WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.360
- Welcome back, everyone.

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.540
My name is Heinrich.

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:03.970
This is my channel, Firm Learning,

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.641
when I talk about what
it takes to be successful

00:00:06.641 --> 00:00:09.580
in the first years of your career.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.880
And today, you'll find me at
a bit of an unusual place.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.250
I'm currently in our
flat in Italy, in Milan.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.360
For those of you who don't
know, I'm half Italian.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.180
So parts of my family
still lives in Italy,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.600
here in Milan, to be precise,

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.480
which is why here I'm enjoying the weekend

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.470
with members of the
family, visiting the city,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:31.010
visiting the university, where
I spent a semester abroad

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.720
during my undergraduate years here,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.770
the Bocconi Business School in Milan.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.180
Great memories, for sure.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.550
A great experience.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.270
But today, what this video
is going to be all about

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.790
is talking about the challenges,

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:46.320
the questions that you might
have as a young professional.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.000
So as someone who is in the first years

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.830
of his or her career, I know
that this can be difficult.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.310
I experienced this myself

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:57.790
when I was in my first years
as a fellow business analyst

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.900
and associate working for the
consulting firm, McKinsey.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.610
Surely this is not always easy.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.040
So I asked you on Instagram

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.360
what the biggest challenges
and questions are

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.360
that you face during the
time, so let's get into that.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.100
I will do my best to give you
some perspectives on these.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.130
Now, based on the survey
I did on Instagram,

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.280
I've got several questions and challenges

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.670
from people like you saying
that their colleagues or clients

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.560
telling that you are too young.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.970
So how to get over that?

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.540
What is my experience?

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:27.060
And yes, I can relate to that

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.580
because I did have several instances

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.030
while working in consulting
and also later in the job

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:33.860
where there were people on the team

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.420
that were not really that
well-accepted by clients,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.608
that got comments,
especially in the beginning,

00:01:38.608 --> 00:01:40.610
especially due to their age.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.050
And often, it's not even
due to their actual age,

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.135
but often just also to the perceived age.

00:01:45.135 --> 00:01:48.150
Of course, it doesn't
help to look very young.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.770
Hear my experiences, though,

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.780
that over the course of working together

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.040
over the course of such a relationship,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.800
you can overcome this
just by being helpful

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.590
and really adding value to the client.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.009
And indeed, what you can
do as a junior person

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:07.650
is often not so much winning
with industry domain expertise.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.220
Of course, you will often
not be the biggest expert

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.000
of what is going in an industry.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.510
You will not have already seen it all,

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:17.260
but what often the skill is
that you can bring to the table,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.240
especially in the consulting context,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.660
especially if you're
already analytically minded

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.350
is really the power of data.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.720
So it's really difficult
to argue with you,

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.210
even if you're very young and
you look very inexperienced,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.470
if you're sitting in a
meeting and you conducted

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.910
a well-researched,
well-carried out analysis,

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.620
and now you're confronting
people with facts,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.630
with numbers, with data.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.770
If you do this well, if
you present this well,

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.840
I mean, it's very difficult
to try argue with you.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.300
And if you show that you can do that,

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.620
if you show that you
really have some insights,

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.908
that you can contribute, by doing this,

00:02:50.908 --> 00:02:54.020
you will get taken seriously.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.480
The next one is to ask
the right questions.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.840
So maybe exactly because you're
not that experienced yet,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:00.673
sometimes you may struggle

00:03:00.673 --> 00:03:03.880
to really come up with
good and helpful questions.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.770
You wonder, maybe, how can
you contribute in meetings?

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:09.840
And you want sneaky little
tip that I picked up over time

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.340
is that especially if you
have an important meeting,

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.840
you are maybe a junior,

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.100
having a document that is being presented,

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.480
think about this in advance.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.620
Go over the document and
look at some good pages

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.280
and think about what things would be

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.125
that you would be able to ask in a meeting

00:03:27.125 --> 00:03:29.130
that would be really insightful,

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.710
that really maybe also
would make you look good,

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.390
make you look smart.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.260
And then at the right times,
if the situation allows it,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.110
ask for these things.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.900
And independent of how you see it,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.510
certainly if you prepare a bit,

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.670
have a bit more time in advance,

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:46.940
it will be much easier for
you really to add value,

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.578
ask the right things,
ask meaningful questions

00:03:49.578 --> 00:03:52.390
than if you just sit in the
meeting not really prepared

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.290
and you come up with things
like that on the spot.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:57.880
The next one is to how to
interact with managers,

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.138
colleagues, and clients at
the beginning of a new job

00:04:00.138 --> 00:04:02.616
because they don't know
you don't know them,

00:04:02.616 --> 00:04:04.750
so how you interact with them.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.210
Surely here's now lots of
things that can be said

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:09.630
on all different types of nuances to that.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.530
One tip that I want to share

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:12.700
that I think makes a lot of sense

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:14.000
and really can make a difference

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.400
is that especially in the first weeks

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.910
on a new product in consulting

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.461
or in the first months
if you work in industry,

00:04:20.461 --> 00:04:24.360
really try to give 200%, give 300%.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.200
Go above and beyond

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.150
because my experience
is if you've established

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.985
this reputation in the beginning,

00:04:30.985 --> 00:04:33.360
that you are really this superstar,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.630
that you are this rockstar consultant,

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:38.420
that you are this like top
one-percenter per year,

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:40.540
if this is the reputation

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.640
that you established in the beginning,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.500
this will go a very long way.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.590
Maybe unable to continue
like this forever,

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.080
and of course, that's okay,

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.505
but I would absolutely be
cautious of is where to start

00:04:50.505 --> 00:04:53.610
rather than the opposite,
to start a bit slower,

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.490
maybe a bit insecure, not doing that much.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.710
This often is difficult

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.720
because then you have the opposite effect,

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.139
then to recover from that and
turn this reputation around

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:06.140
is really then a long
way that you need to go.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.600
Next thing is about dealing
with imposter syndrome.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.530
So what do you do if you feel like

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.160
you really have imposter syndrome,

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.470
that you don't really deserve to be here,

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.220
that it feels like all these other people

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.070
are so much smarter than you are?

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.110
And I for sure remember this.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.900
I very, very remember the first weeks

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.372
on my very first project in consulting.

00:05:25.372 --> 00:05:28.000
I felt like I should not be here.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.810
I felt like I was working
together on a team

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.880
with other associates, other consultants,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.830
and these people seemed
so above and beyond,

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.230
so much smarter, so
much more capable of me.

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:42.370
I really remember after
the first weeks, wondering,

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.873
asking myself whether I am the
right person to do this job,

00:05:45.873 --> 00:05:47.524
whether I would, at any point,

00:05:47.524 --> 00:05:52.130
be able to be only half
as good as these people.

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.900
But the truth is the
learning curve is quite steep

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.510
and what you will only see
is that where these are,

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.210
maybe one year tenure, two years tenure,

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.837
this is such a development
step from where you are

00:06:02.837 --> 00:06:04.660
or someone who just started out

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.690
compared to someone who's
already doing all of this

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.320
after one or two years,

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.290
because what you don't
realize in the beginning

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:14.473
is that there are lots
of little tips and tricks

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:17.948
that might make you to be
perceived as very confident,

00:06:17.948 --> 00:06:22.670
very knowledgeable, even if
maybe in this specific moment,

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.010
you are not necessarily that confident

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:26.750
and that well knowledgeable, right?

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.950
And actually, I even did
a video about this topic

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:30.740
a couple of weeks or months ago.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:32.841
I will link at somewhere above here,

00:06:32.841 --> 00:06:35.388
where I share some of these
sneaky little tips and tricks,

00:06:35.388 --> 00:06:37.443
what consultants, and of
course, also many other people

00:06:37.443 --> 00:06:40.890
that are outside of consulting
sometimes do to really create

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.400
this very positive
perception about themselves,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.560
which maybe is not really always backed up

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.350
with hard knowledge, right?

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:49.800
And of course, that's just
part of the game as well.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.120
You will see this in all different types

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.480
of professional services context,

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.340
and for sure also in industry,

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:55.900
but learning these little things

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:56.870
and then of course over time,

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:59.470
also getting the actual
hard industry knowledge,

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:02.000
by all of this, it's very
likely that in one or two years,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.122
you will suddenly be this
quite experienced consultant,

00:07:05.122 --> 00:07:08.216
associate who works
together with a new joiner,

00:07:08.216 --> 00:07:09.500
and trust me,

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:11.866
this new joiner very
likely will look up to you

00:07:11.866 --> 00:07:13.900
and think the very same things

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.770
that you thought in the beginning.

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:17.900
So I guess just trust the process.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:21.164
Of course, learn, be
open-minded, learn the game,

00:07:21.164 --> 00:07:23.950
learn the skills, and you will be fine.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.010
The next one is about setting boundaries.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:28.869
How can you, even if you work
in a high-paced environment

00:07:28.869 --> 00:07:31.860
with of course, long
hours, lots of pressure,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:35.070
how do you succeed to set boundaries

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:38.010
to also still carve out
some time for yourself?

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.460
And of course, this is
often especially challenging

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.210
in professional services like consulting,

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.582
probably even more in banking,

00:07:43.582 --> 00:07:46.260
where just so much is expected from you.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.810
But hear also my experiences.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.940
The people often that struggled most

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.530
were really the new
joiners, the junior people

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.730
who are only in the firm
for a couple of months,

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.330
and then often, these people feel like

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:58.370
they need to do everything
they're asked to do.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:00.720
They always need to say
yes, whatever it takes,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.020
but then it was often the bit
more experienced associates,

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.850
one year, second year,
third year associate

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:08.670
who knew the game a little bit better

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:12.070
and that did manage quite well
also to fence off some time,

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:13.440
set these boundaries,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.060
and thereby also manage
their work-life balance.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.100
And it did how to
achieve this, my tidbits,

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.500
that it's very fair to, for
instance, in the beginning,

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.560
have a conversation with
your project leader,

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.200
with your team to
industry, wherever you work

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.920
that you very clearly state

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.150
what is important to you
also regarding lifestyle.

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.130
And of course, it's
important to be realistic.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.340
So like if you work for
an NBB consulting firm

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.380
like McKinsey, BCG, or Bain,
if you tell this project lead

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.510
that you just expect that every evening,

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:41.670
you are done with work at 8:00 PM,

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.700
I mean, this is just completely
not realistic, right?

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:44.820
So this will not work.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.480
But what will work is that
maybe you tell this project lead

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:48.970
that it's important to you

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:52.130
that at least one evening during the week,

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.840
then at least a bit earlier
at 8:00 or 9:00 PM or so,

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.650
you have this evening to
yourself, having flexibility,

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:00.740
for instance, to go to the
hotel, hit the hotel gym,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.103
and then if there's more work to do,

00:09:02.103 --> 00:09:04.020
then maybe afterwards
you can do that, right?

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.160
Just as an example.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.758
Or maybe you state that
it's very important to you

00:09:07.758 --> 00:09:10.483
that every lunchtime, you get a warm meal,

00:09:10.483 --> 00:09:12.780
because maybe this is
really what matters to you.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.170
You feel like if you don't
have proper lunch every day,

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.570
then you struggle to
really do your job well.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:19.565
I mean, clearly state
expectations like this

00:09:19.565 --> 00:09:21.650
and if you clearly communicate this

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:22.856
and then also act on that,

00:09:22.856 --> 00:09:25.720
then my experience is
everyone will understand,

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:26.750
everyone will be okay

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.160
if they're maybe even a
bit earlier than the rest.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.260
Maybe the rest of the team

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:32.470
is then still working from the team room,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.397
but then you leave a bit
earlier on that specific day

00:09:35.397 --> 00:09:37.120
to hit the gym

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.453
because you clearly said that
this is important to you.

00:09:39.453 --> 00:09:42.010
Another thing that several
of you asked is my opinion

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.220
on the point of specialization
versus generalization.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:46.560
So at the beginning of your career,

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.730
should you rather choose a
path that leads you towards

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:51.190
a general experiencing
lots of different things,

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.410
a more specialized one where
you build deep domain knowledge

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:55.760
in a specific area?

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.269
And here, certainly both paths
can lead to great success

00:09:58.269 --> 00:10:00.213
and I trust you can build a great career

00:10:00.213 --> 00:10:02.360
based on both of the types of things.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.190
In my humble opinion, I do think
that there's a lot of value

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:06.940
in that in the beginning of your career,

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.170
really follow the generalist approach.

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:10.395
See lots of different things.

00:10:10.395 --> 00:10:11.501
This is, of course,

00:10:11.501 --> 00:10:14.740
what you will be able to do
in many consulting roles,

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.397
so you'll start in a generalist track

00:10:16.397 --> 00:10:18.700
and then in the first
one, two, or three years,

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.970
have the opportunity to see
lots of different industries,

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:23.660
lots of different functions.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:25.800
I think this has two main benefits.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:26.710
First, of course,

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.283
by exposing yourself to many
of these different areas,

00:10:29.283 --> 00:10:30.630
probably this will help you

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.650
to make a more informed decision

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:34.796
which area maybe at a
later stage in your career,

00:10:34.796 --> 00:10:37.800
you already want to focus
on, but then second,

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:40.260
and this is really where I'm
also drawing from right now

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.880
and having worked after
consulting in a retail role,

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.920
right now in a startup
role in prop tech company,

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.410
after working in all
these different functions

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:51.120
and industries, getting
this broad experience,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:52.793
what this helps a bit is confidence,

00:10:52.793 --> 00:10:55.810
confidence that you are able to navigate

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:57.393
lots of different environments,

00:10:57.393 --> 00:11:02.320
that you are able to add value
in many, many different areas

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.597
and instances of work.

00:11:03.597 --> 00:11:06.840
And this confidence, I think,
is very easy to undervalue,

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:07.970
to underestimate.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.870
I think it's quite an asset
that you have the mindset,

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:14.030
that yes, more or less
independent of what the setting is

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.260
that you will enter in
your professional life,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:17.940
also after professional services

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:19.810
if you're anti-industry at some point,

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:21.930
doesn't matter what area exactly you work,

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.300
and you learn some tools

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:25.998
to make you successful in this area,

00:11:25.998 --> 00:11:29.870
you have the confidence
that, yes, you can add value.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:32.840
I would not underestimate then
what the value of this is.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:34.230
I do think, at least for me personally,

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.610
it made quite a difference.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:36.780
And if you have the opportunity

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.143
to collect this more
generalist experience,

00:11:39.143 --> 00:11:41.499
I do think that it's
worthwhile to follow that

00:11:41.499 --> 00:11:43.960
because then always after some time,

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.880
there's still lots of
opportunities to specialize.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:48.030
But to the contrary,

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:50.721
if you're already specialized
in a quite significant way

00:11:50.721 --> 00:11:53.030
in the first years of your career,

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:54.745
I would argue it's then quite difficult

00:11:54.745 --> 00:11:59.020
to then move towards a more
general manager type of path,

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:00.120
to move towards a path

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.300
where then you do see different things.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.380
So do this in the beginning.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:06.000
I think this is, in my humble
opinion, the sweet spot.

